00:49:14.017 --> 00:49:15.552
Do you think there's anything

00:49:15.552 --> 00:49:16.954
though the government could do?

00:49:16.954 --> 00:49:18.455
Let's say we had a government,

00:49:18.455 --> 00:49:19.623
certainly not the
government we have

00:49:19.623 --> 00:49:20.991
at the federal level now

00:49:20.991 --> 00:49:22.559 align:start size:97% position:3%
but if there was a government
to come in and say,

00:49:22.559 --> 00:49:25.362 align:start size:84% position:16%
okay, we want to
address these issues

00:49:25.362 --> 00:49:28.765
around the family, around
generations on welfare,

00:49:28.765 --> 00:49:30.500 align:start size:84% position:16%
all of these things
we want to address it.

00:49:30.500 --> 00:49:31.935
What do you do?

00:49:31.935 --> 00:49:33.603 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, I've heard some
people like Candace Owens say,

00:49:33.603 --> 00:49:35.939 align:start size:84% position:16%
you just stopped the
programs tomorrow

00:49:35.939 --> 00:49:37.975
and you let that pain point hit.

00:49:37.975 --> 00:49:40.310
Do you think there's a
sensible policy there?

00:49:40.310 --> 00:49:42.079
Do you say, just end everything?

00:49:42.079 --> 00:49:45.382 align:start size:88% position:13%
What's the actual
action that can happen?

00:49:46.984 --> 00:49:49.720
- Government cannot
do social uplift,

00:49:49.720 --> 00:49:51.888
it doesn't know how to do it.

00:49:51.888 --> 00:49:54.191
And of course right now
you have government,

00:49:54.191 --> 00:49:58.595 align:start position:0%
the social work schools are
cranking out government workers

00:49:58.595 --> 00:50:01.665 align:start size:94% position:6%
that are utterly committed
to non-judgmentalism

00:50:01.665 --> 00:50:03.800
when it comes to self
destructive behavior.

00:50:03.800 --> 00:50:06.803
Like I remember in
the 90's in New York,

00:50:06.803 --> 00:50:09.606
I used to write a lot
about welfare reform

00:50:09.606 --> 00:50:13.710 align:start size:88% position:13%
and would talk to these
non-profit workers

00:50:13.710 --> 00:50:16.680
and they were adamant
that they couldn't say

00:50:16.680 --> 00:50:19.249
a word about out of
wedlock childbirth

00:50:19.249 --> 00:50:22.352 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is the biggest predictor
of poverty in this country.

00:50:22.352 --> 00:50:24.988
If we could wipe out out
of wedlock childbearing,

00:50:24.988 --> 00:50:28.425
reunite families together
we'd get rid of poverty

00:50:28.425 --> 00:50:30.961
and we'd get rid
of almost all crime

00:50:30.961 --> 00:50:33.730
and it's it's happening now in
the white community as well.

00:50:33.730 --> 00:50:38.668
The Hillbilly Elegy
population as well.

00:50:38.668 --> 00:50:40.604
Charles Murray has
written about that.

00:50:40.604 --> 00:50:42.639
So government does not know

00:50:42.639 --> 00:50:46.343 align:start size:84% position:16%
how to change
people's upbringing.

00:50:46.343 --> 00:50:48.678
So yes, it would
be a great thing.

00:50:48.678 --> 00:50:52.115 align:start position:0%
I would absolutely back Candace
Owens of going cold turkey

00:50:52.115 --> 00:50:54.951 align:start size:84% position:16%
and there were
proposals about that

00:50:56.253 --> 00:51:00.624 align:start size:88% position:13%
during the welfare
reform era of the 1990's

00:51:00.624 --> 00:51:03.393 align:start size:84% position:16%
of at least not
giving more checks,

00:51:03.393 --> 00:51:06.530
larger checks when people had
more children out of wedlock

00:51:06.530 --> 00:51:08.498
but that was a very hard sell.

00:51:08.498 --> 00:51:11.935 align:start size:88% position:13%
I would say government,
what it's there for

00:51:11.935 --> 00:51:16.273 align:start size:97% position:3%
is providing public order,
providing the basis for people

00:51:16.273 --> 00:51:18.075
to exercise their own initiative

00:51:18.075 --> 00:51:20.677
that is above all safe streets

00:51:20.677 --> 00:51:22.846
so that businesses
don't have to worry

00:51:22.846 --> 00:51:25.148
about their employees,
they can set up shop

00:51:25.148 --> 00:51:28.251
and people can thrive and get

00:51:28.251 --> 00:51:30.287
where they need to
go without fear.

00:51:30.287 --> 00:51:31.755
- You're speaking my language.

00:51:31.755 --> 00:51:33.657
It's so interesting what
you said about in the '90's

00:51:33.657 --> 00:51:35.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the social workers
and they couldn't bring up

00:51:35.659 --> 00:51:37.327
the one parent household.

00:51:37.327 --> 00:51:39.262 align:start size:94% position:6%
It reminds me of something
that David Horowitz

00:51:39.262 --> 00:51:41.198 align:start size:84% position:16%
who I'm sure you know
have told me once

00:51:41.198 --> 00:51:44.367
when he was still a lefty
in New York City politics

00:51:44.367 --> 00:51:47.737 align:start size:88% position:13%
in the early '80's
during the AIDS crisis,

00:51:47.737 --> 00:51:50.774
they or he was just
sort of becoming

00:51:50.774 --> 00:51:52.142
a Republican in essence.

00:51:52.142 --> 00:51:54.044 align:start size:81% position:19%
And they knew that
the AIDS crisis

00:51:54.044 --> 00:51:57.314
that in effect ground zero
was these gay bathhouses

00:51:57.314 --> 00:51:59.382 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause people were
having all kinds of sex

00:51:59.382 --> 00:52:01.184
and doing drugs and
all sorts of stuff.

00:52:01.184 --> 00:52:05.489
And the Republicans wanted
to close down the bath houses

00:52:05.489 --> 00:52:07.157
but the Democrats said, no,

00:52:07.157 --> 00:52:10.660 align:start size:97% position:3%
you're a bunch of homophobes
and you hate gay people

00:52:10.660 --> 00:52:13.730 align:start size:97% position:3%
thus they actually extended
the crisis probably

00:52:13.730 --> 00:52:15.732
for years and years after that

00:52:15.732 --> 00:52:19.002 align:start position:0%
because it's just, it's the
same culture of fear in essence.

00:52:20.437 --> 00:52:24.708 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- Well, I've said the big
divide between conservatives

00:52:24.708 --> 00:52:28.445 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
and liberals or
progressives you left is

00:52:28.445 --> 00:52:30.714
whether you see behavior

00:52:30.714 --> 00:52:34.651 align:start size:97% position:3%
as the most important
determinant of life outcomes

00:52:34.651 --> 00:52:38.855 align:start size:97% position:3%
or you see large systemic
structures as the determinant.

00:52:38.855 --> 00:52:42.125
And I'm sure there's
counter examples here

00:52:42.125 --> 00:52:46.663 align:start size:97% position:3%
but by and large, a liberal
is gonna say but look,

00:52:46.663 --> 00:52:51.568 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's these huge structures,
homophobia or racism

00:52:51.568 --> 00:52:54.471 align:start size:84% position:16%
that prevent people
from thriving.

00:52:54.471 --> 00:52:57.007
And again, in the case of AIDS,

00:52:57.007 --> 00:52:59.509 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean it was just remarkable
that we were not allowed

00:52:59.509 --> 00:53:04.414 align:start size:94% position:6%
to talk about grotesquely
promiscuous sex.

00:53:05.815 --> 00:53:08.785
And pretending that AIDS
is like an airborne disease

00:53:08.785 --> 00:53:13.056 align:start size:97% position:3%
and infectious disease as
opposed to something that is

00:53:13.056 --> 00:53:17.928
almost 100% avoidable by
not doing intravenous drugs

00:53:17.928 --> 00:53:21.565
and not engaging
in anonymous sex

00:53:21.565 --> 00:53:25.368
with people who you have
no idea what their STDs

00:53:25.368 --> 00:53:26.703
status is or anything.

00:53:26.703 --> 00:53:28.271
You can avoid it 100%.

00:53:28.271 --> 00:53:32.676 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so a conservatives
is gonna focus on

00:53:32.676 --> 00:53:35.312 align:start size:88% position:13%
those behavioral
choices that people make

00:53:35.312 --> 00:53:38.815 align:start size:88% position:13%
and be more skeptical
and a liberal would say,

00:53:38.815 --> 00:53:43.053
how can you be so blind to the
systemic structural barriers?

00:53:43.053 --> 00:53:47.390
And that's, again, it's one of
those constitutional divides

00:53:47.390 --> 00:53:51.661
in how we look at the world
that is very hard to bridge.

00:53:51.661 --> 00:53:54.664 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Do you I think the more
and more you do these things

00:53:54.664 --> 00:53:56.600 align:start size:97% position:3%
that you study these things,
talk about these things

00:53:56.600 --> 00:53:58.568
and the more we've seen so much

00:53:58.568 --> 00:54:01.104
of what you talk
about come true.

00:54:01.104 --> 00:54:03.540 align:start size:97% position:3%
Do you ultimately think that
it's partly just the way

00:54:03.540 --> 00:54:05.775
our brains are wired at the end

00:54:05.775 --> 00:54:08.979 align:start size:97% position:3%
that it's just some
people's fear center is this

00:54:08.979 --> 00:54:10.981 align:start size:88% position:13%
and some people's other
fear center is that

00:54:10.981 --> 00:54:14.784
so the mask then, if for some
people you walk around and go,

00:54:14.784 --> 00:54:16.019
man, these people are bananas

00:54:16.019 --> 00:54:17.320
that's sort of where I'm at

00:54:17.320 --> 00:54:19.356
but then someone else
thinks it actually

00:54:19.356 --> 00:54:23.226
in some ways giving them
purpose in a bizarre sense.

00:54:23.226 --> 00:54:24.728
- That's absolutely right.

00:54:24.728 --> 00:54:26.963 align:start size:97% position:3%
It does give you purpose and
you feel like you're part

00:54:26.963 --> 00:54:30.467
of something bigger
and fear is as I say,

00:54:30.467 --> 00:54:32.035
it gives you feel
like you're alive

00:54:32.035 --> 00:54:34.037
and it also gives you virtue.

00:54:34.037 --> 00:54:37.641 align:start size:97% position:3%
Yeah, I do and I also
think we're all susceptible

00:54:37.641 --> 00:54:41.044 align:start size:84% position:16%
to enormous cognitive
blindnesses,

00:54:41.044 --> 00:54:46.049 align:start size:97% position:3%
and so I'm sure conservatives
are just as inconsistent

00:54:48.418 --> 00:54:49.953 align:start size:84% position:16%
in their application
of principles.

00:54:49.953 --> 00:54:53.623
So I mean, we've all seen the
sort of seesaw go up and down

00:54:53.623 --> 00:54:55.992
as to whether one's in
favor of executive orders

00:54:55.992 --> 00:54:59.029
based on whether your
guy's in office or not.

00:54:59.029 --> 00:55:01.197
So I do kind of despair.

00:55:01.197 --> 00:55:06.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I have to say,
and this is going to anger

00:55:06.036 --> 00:55:09.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
a lot of people and
alienated a lot of people

00:55:09.039 --> 00:55:14.044
but I have been depressed
by seeing the willingness

00:55:16.713 --> 00:55:20.784
of so many people on
the right to embrace

00:55:20.784 --> 00:55:23.520
what I think are just
completely ridiculous

00:55:23.520 --> 00:55:28.158 align:start position:0%
conspiracy theories with regards
to dominion voting systems

00:55:28.158 --> 00:55:31.328
and to not apply the Hume test,

00:55:31.328 --> 00:55:36.333
the test for miracles of
having a basic skepticism

00:55:37.734 --> 00:55:40.036
towards claims which ask
is it more likely than not?

00:55:40.036 --> 00:55:42.372
Is it more likely than not

00:55:42.372 --> 00:55:45.875
that there was a
systemic conspiracy

00:55:45.875 --> 00:55:48.645 align:start size:94% position:6%
across all levels of
government in this country

00:55:48.645 --> 00:55:50.647
to destroy Trump votes?

00:55:50.647 --> 00:55:54.584
To me that it's a step too far

00:55:54.584 --> 00:55:59.589 align:start size:84% position:16%
but people have
selective rationality.

00:56:00.757 --> 00:56:03.059 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so I think that kind
of a libertarian view

00:56:03.059 --> 00:56:05.662
that views everybody is it's
all gonna come out all right

00:56:05.662 --> 00:56:08.565
in the wash because
we're all rational.

00:56:08.565 --> 00:56:10.066
It's not quite the way it works.

00:56:10.066 --> 00:56:12.068
And we've had people
like Daniel Conaman

00:56:12.068 --> 00:56:14.571
for years now point out sort

00:56:17.340 --> 00:56:21.945 align:start size:97% position:3%
of failures of how human
beings are wired cognitively

00:56:21.945 --> 00:56:26.950 align:start size:97% position:3%
and tendency towards see
agency where it doesn't exist.

00:56:28.351 --> 00:56:30.253 align:start size:97% position:3%
I would say that's part of
the religious impulse as well.

00:56:30.253 --> 00:56:34.858 align:start size:97% position:3%
So one can only hope and
make those arguments as often

00:56:34.858 --> 00:56:37.627
as one can from one's own
perspective of rationality

00:56:37.627 --> 00:56:40.864
knowing that we are
I am fallible too.

00:56:40.864 --> 00:56:44.701
And I think we need some
epistemological humility

00:56:46.169 --> 00:56:50.273
towards our own positions
even as we're criticizing

00:56:50.273 --> 00:56:54.010 align:start size:94% position:6%
what appear to be complete
insanity on the side

00:56:54.010 --> 00:56:56.913
of ideological opponents.

00:56:56.913 --> 00:57:00.049 align:start size:84% position:16%
- That probably is the
right place to stop

00:57:00.049 --> 00:57:01.384
because it was a
beautiful ending

00:57:01.384 --> 00:57:03.153 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I'm going to
push you one further

00:57:03.153 --> 00:57:05.555
which is that my audience knows

00:57:05.555 --> 00:57:10.560 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I'm really trying to help
people just not feel crazy

00:57:10.560 --> 00:57:13.696
about all of this 'cause the
world really does feel crazy.

00:57:13.696 --> 00:57:16.766 align:start size:88% position:13%
We're watching the
close of the old world,

00:57:16.766 --> 00:57:19.035 align:start size:88% position:13%
we're watching the
beginning of a new world

00:57:19.035 --> 00:57:22.439
that is going to feel very
different no matter what.

00:57:24.007 --> 00:57:27.143
I sense you're probably similar

00:57:27.143 --> 00:57:28.411
to the way I think of myself.

00:57:28.411 --> 00:57:30.780
I consider myself a
world weary optimist

00:57:30.780 --> 00:57:33.416
but really a real list
more than anything else.

00:57:33.416 --> 00:57:35.919 align:start position:0%
I think you probably, you
certainly must consider yourself

00:57:35.919 --> 00:57:40.190 align:start size:94% position:6%
a realist but can you
track some sort of future

00:57:40.190 --> 00:57:42.659 align:start size:88% position:13%
that looks okay over the
next couple of years?

00:57:42.659 --> 00:57:44.327
Like is there something
that you can sort of see

00:57:44.327 --> 00:57:45.862
in the distance that's like, oh,

00:57:45.862 --> 00:57:48.932 align:start position:0%
we can get through this without
the complete destruction

00:57:48.932 --> 00:57:51.734
of everything that
you know is good.

00:57:53.903 --> 00:57:56.239
- The truth, I mean, I just

00:57:56.239 --> 00:57:58.374 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I'm not a world
very optimist,

00:57:58.374 --> 00:57:59.676 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm a world very pessimistic.
(interviewer laughing)

00:57:59.676 --> 00:58:01.211
Again, this to constitution,

00:58:01.211 --> 00:58:03.880
I can tell, I'm not
an optimist by nature

00:58:03.880 --> 00:58:05.949 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I know that clouds
how I see things

00:58:05.949 --> 00:58:07.951
and I find optimist crazy like,

00:58:07.951 --> 00:58:09.419
how can you guys think
it's gonna get better?

00:58:09.419 --> 00:58:11.454
It's not, but let's
pretend, but no,

00:58:11.454 --> 00:58:15.258 align:start size:97% position:3%
there are ways, the only way
out of this is the truth,

00:58:15.258 --> 00:58:17.961
do not be cowed by
the charge of racism.

00:58:17.961 --> 00:58:22.031
I do believe that the
core problem in all
of our world today

00:58:22.031 --> 00:58:27.036 align:start size:97% position:3%
is the racial gap, the idea
that America is racist,

00:58:28.404 --> 00:58:31.674 align:start size:97% position:3%
white supremacy that's
driving an enormous portion

00:58:31.674 --> 00:58:33.309
of the current insanity.

00:58:33.309 --> 00:58:37.881 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have to speak the truth
about accomplishment,

00:58:37.881 --> 00:58:42.886 align:start size:84% position:16%
about culture and
not allow our history

00:58:44.020 --> 00:58:45.989 align:start size:84% position:16%
and our civilization
to be torn down

00:58:45.989 --> 00:58:50.660 align:start size:94% position:6%
based on hatred,
resentment and mediocrity.

00:58:52.462 --> 00:58:55.098
Stick up for what you love.

00:58:55.098 --> 00:58:58.902
I'm gonna stick up for
the art that I think is

00:58:58.902 --> 00:59:03.740
is greater than any of us
have any right to deserve,

00:59:03.740 --> 00:59:07.243 align:start size:84% position:16%
we do not deserve
the beauty of Chopin

00:59:07.243 --> 00:59:09.679 align:start size:84% position:16%
and Brahms piano music
and Mozart Operas.

00:59:09.679 --> 00:59:12.215
None of us have done
anything to justify

00:59:12.215 --> 00:59:15.351
this cornucopia of sublimity.

00:59:15.351 --> 00:59:16.986
Keep fighting for it.

00:59:16.986 --> 00:59:19.556
Keep fighting for what
you believe with respect,

00:59:19.556 --> 00:59:24.327 align:start position:0%
not violence that's all you
can do is just go down fighting

00:59:24.327 --> 00:59:25.595
at the very least.

00:59:25.595 --> 00:59:27.263 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Heather Mac Donald,
you are truly one

00:59:27.263 --> 00:59:28.531
of my favorite guests.

00:59:28.531 --> 00:59:30.533
Your most recent book is
the "Diversity Delusion"

00:59:30.533 --> 00:59:32.936
